<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Rewa State</h1><p class="st-meta">1236 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Rewa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Baghel Rajputs, which gave its name to Baghelkhand, sent the singer Tansen to Akbar's court and whose Maharaja captured Mohan, ancestor of most captive white tigers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Baghel Rajputs</a>, a branch of the Solanki clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, moved into the region in the 13th century; tradition credits Vyaghra Deva with the founding. Their early stronghold was the hill fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandhavgarh">Bandhogarh</a> (Bandhavgarh).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramchandra-singh-of-rewa">Ramchandra</a> was patron of the musician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a>, who left his court for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar's</a> in the 1560s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandhavgarh">Bandhogarh</a> was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1597, and the capital moved to Rewa. The state was large and rural, with forests rich in game. In 1812 Rewa entered treaty relations with the British, and its rulers later sent troops to help against the rebels of 1857.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1948 Rewa became the leading member and capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a>, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. In 1951 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martand-singh">Martand Singh</a> captured the white tiger Mohan, ancestor of many captive white tigers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rewa-state.jpg" alt="Rewa State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Rewa_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1236 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rewa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ramchandra, Martand Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bagheli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rewa, Baghelkhand</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=rewa-state" aria-label="Rewa State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6840 1275 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rewa State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7160 1531L7364 1601M7160 1531L7364 1602M7160 1531L7160 1530"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7160 1530h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7160 1531h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7160 1531h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rewa. <a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=rewa-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
